  "body": {
    "id": "prop:equal-variance-reduction",
    "kind": "proposition",
    "statement": "If \\(s_i=\\sigma^2\\) for every arm, then \\(w_i=1/K\\), \\(C=2(K-1)/K\\), \\(\\eta=1/(K-1)\\), \\[\\rho^\\star=\\frac{K^2}{2(K-1)^2+K},\\qquad G=\\frac{2K(K-1)}{2(K-1)^2+K}.\\] In particular, \\(K=3\\) gives \\(\\rho^\\star=9/11\\) and \\(G=12/11\\), recovering the common-variance late-elimination improvement.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "s",
      "K",
      "w",
      "C",
      "\\eta",
      "\\rho^\\star",
      "G"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "def:static-comparator",
      "def:projection-coefficient",
      "def:robust-schedule",
      "thm:universal-dominance"
    ],
    "justification": "This mandatory reduction checks the weighted formula against the known symmetric geometry.",
    "gap": "Imbens, Qin, and Wager establish common-variance dominance; the proposition shows that the new weighted theorem contains that regime rather than replacing it by an analogue on a disjoint class.",
    "consumer": "Readers can benchmark the heteroskedastic schedule against the familiar equal-allocation design and reproduce the three-arm gain by hand.",
    "proof_tex": "Suppose \\(s_i=\\sigma^2\\) for all \\(i\\).  Let \\(u_{(1)}\\le u_{(2)}\\) be the two smallest coordinates of an arbitrary interior allocation.  Their sum is at most \\(2/K\\), and therefore\n\\[\n \\max_{i<j}\\sigma^2\\left(\\frac1{u_i}+\\frac1{u_j}\\right)\n \\ge \\sigma^2\\left(\\frac1{u_{(1)}}+\\frac1{u_{(2)}}\\right)\n \\ge \\frac{4\\sigma^2}{u_{(1)}+u_{(2)}}\\ge2K\\sigma^2. \\tag{20}\n\\]\nThe uniform allocation attains (20).  Equality requires \\(u_{(1)}=u_{(2)}=1/K\\); since these are the two smallest coordinates and all coordinates sum to one, every coordinate must then be \\(1/K\\).  Hence the selector in \\(\\text{def:static-comparator}\\) has\n\\[\n w_i=\\frac1K,\\qquad v_i=K\\sigma^2=:V.\n\\]\nThus \\(d_{hj}=2\\sqrt V\\) for every \\(j\\ne h\\), and\n\\[\n F_h(t)=\\frac{t^2}{2V}+\\frac{K-1}{2V}(2\\sqrt V-t)_+^2.\n\\]\nIts minimizer is \\(t=2(K-1)\\sqrt V/K<2\\sqrt V\\), and direct substitution gives\n\\[\n C_h=C=\\frac{2(K-1)}K.\n\\]\nAlso\n\\[\n \\eta=\\frac{1/K}{1-1/K}=\\frac1{K-1}.\n\\]\nSubstitution into \\(\\text{def:robust-schedule}\\) yields\n\\[\n \\rho^\\star\n =\\frac{K^2}{2(K-1)^2+K},\n \\qquad\n G=\\frac{2K(K-1)}{2(K-1)^2+K}.\n\\]\nThe strict dominance conclusion follows from \\(\\text{thm:universal-dominance}\\).  At \\(K=3\\), the formulas reduce to \\(\\rho^\\star=9/11\\) and \\(G=12/11\\).",
